1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,350
Five friends.

2
00:00:01,229 --> 00:00:03,519
Diya must pick two of
them for a quiz team.

3
00:00:03,919 --> 00:00:07,624
Last time, a captain and a
vice captain gave twenty ways.

4
00:00:07,624 --> 00:00:09,304
Now there are no badges.

5
00:00:09,304 --> 00:00:10,687
Is it still twenty?

6
00:00:11,137 --> 00:00:13,873
Diya has to make this choice
for the class quiz today.

7
00:00:14,223 --> 00:00:16,083
Meera and Sana, or Sana and Meera.

8
00:00:16,083 --> 00:00:17,250
It is the same team.

9
00:00:17,250 --> 00:00:18,764
So should we count it twice?

10
00:00:19,264 --> 00:00:20,164
No.

11
00:00:19,630 --> 00:00:22,599
When order does not matter,
we are counting combinations.

12
00:00:23,250 --> 00:00:24,913
So what is a combination?

13
00:00:25,263 --> 00:00:29,856
A combination is a selection of some
or all of the objects, where the order

14
00:00:29,856 --> 00:00:31,032
does not matter.

15
00:00:31,432 --> 00:00:34,065
Selection means we only ask who is in.

16
00:00:34,065 --> 00:00:35,407
Not who is first.

17
00:00:35,807 --> 00:00:38,007
An arrangement asks who goes where.

18
00:00:38,007 --> 00:00:40,123
A selection asks only who is in.

19
00:00:40,773 --> 00:00:43,337
Let us bring back the grid from last time.

20
00:00:43,337 --> 00:00:44,330
Twenty squares.

21
00:00:44,330 --> 00:00:46,849
Each one is a captain and a vice captain.

22
00:00:47,249 --> 00:00:48,953
Look at Meera captain, Sana vice.

23
00:00:48,953 --> 00:00:50,469
And Sana captain, Meera vice.

24
00:00:50,469 --> 00:00:52,072
As a team, they are the same.

25
00:00:52,472 --> 00:00:55,749
Every square has a mirror
twin across the diagonal.

26
00:00:55,749 --> 00:00:59,134
Fold the grid, and each
twin lands on its partner.

27
00:00:59,734 --> 00:01:01,912
Twenty squares become ten teams.

28
00:01:01,912 --> 00:01:04,150
Each team was counted two times.

29
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:05,721
Side by side.

30
00:01:05,721 --> 00:01:08,697
Arrangement: Meera then
Sana is not Sana then Meera.

31
00:01:08,697 --> 00:01:09,840
That is two ways.

32
00:01:10,240 --> 00:01:14,083
Selection: Meera and Sana is one
team, whichever way you say it.

33
00:01:14,083 --> 00:01:15,217
That is one way.

34
00:01:15,817 --> 00:01:16,729
Now you try.

35
00:01:16,729 --> 00:01:18,862
Choosing three toppings for a pizza.

36
00:01:18,862 --> 00:01:20,481
Arrangement, or selection?

37
00:01:20,481 --> 00:01:21,614
Pause and decide.

38
00:01:24,814 --> 00:01:25,714
Selection!

39
00:01:25,602 --> 00:01:29,398
The pizza tastes the same,
whichever topping goes on first.

40
00:01:30,048 --> 00:01:31,084
Now the rule.

41
00:01:31,434 --> 00:01:33,800
Choose r things from n different things.

42
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:35,652
First, count the arrangements.

43
00:01:35,652 --> 00:01:36,760
That is n P r.

44
00:01:37,510 --> 00:01:41,880
But each chosen group can be arranged
among itself in r factorial ways.

45
00:01:42,630 --> 00:01:45,980
So every group was
counted r factorial times.

46
00:01:45,980 --> 00:01:48,003
We divide by r factorial.

47
00:01:48,753 --> 00:01:50,566
This is written n C r.

48
00:01:50,566 --> 00:01:52,687
The C stands for combination.

49
00:01:53,437 --> 00:01:56,726
n C r equals n P r over r factorial.

50
00:01:56,726 --> 00:02:02,026
That is n factorial, over r
factorial times n minus r factorial.

51
00:02:02,776 --> 00:02:03,676
Check it.

52
00:02:03,594 --> 00:02:05,758
Five C two is twenty over two.

53
00:02:05,758 --> 00:02:06,658
Ten teams.

54
00:02:07,275 --> 00:02:10,074
Watch the count change as the team grows.

55
00:02:10,074 --> 00:02:11,101
Five friends.

56
00:02:11,501 --> 00:02:14,326
A team of zero: one way, pick nobody.

57
00:02:14,326 --> 00:02:16,318
A team of one: five ways.

58
00:02:16,318 --> 00:02:17,945
A team of two: ten.

59
00:02:18,345 --> 00:02:20,367
A team of three: ten again.

60
00:02:20,367 --> 00:02:21,673
Four: five ways.

61
00:02:21,673 --> 00:02:23,136
All five: one way.

62
00:02:23,736 --> 00:02:24,636
Quick one.

63
00:02:24,571 --> 00:02:27,308
Why is five C two equal to five C three?

64
00:02:27,308 --> 00:02:28,489
Pause and think.

65
00:02:31,689 --> 00:02:35,940
Picking two to play is the same
as picking three to sit out.

66
00:02:35,940 --> 00:02:38,578
So n C r equals n C, n minus r.

67
00:02:39,227 --> 00:02:40,145
Remember this.

68
00:02:40,145 --> 00:02:43,885
When order does not matter, divide
the arrangements by r factorial.

69
00:02:44,585 --> 00:02:46,274
Now, one worked example.

70
00:02:46,624 --> 00:02:47,544
Eight friends.

71
00:02:47,544 --> 00:02:49,323
Only three can go on a trip.

72
00:02:49,323 --> 00:02:51,764
In how many ways can the three be chosen?

73
00:02:52,264 --> 00:02:53,164
Step one.

74
00:02:53,062 --> 00:02:55,523
A trip group has no first or second.

75
00:02:55,523 --> 00:02:57,069
Order does not matter.

76
00:02:57,069 --> 00:02:58,616
This is a combination.

77
00:02:59,366 --> 00:03:00,266
Step two.

78
00:03:00,178 --> 00:03:01,604
n is eight friends.

79
00:03:01,604 --> 00:03:02,981
r is three places.

80
00:03:03,731 --> 00:03:04,664
Step three.

81
00:03:04,664 --> 00:03:09,602
Eight C three is eight factorial, over
three factorial times five factorial.

82
00:03:10,352 --> 00:03:11,252
Step four.

83
00:03:11,206 --> 00:03:15,851
That leaves eight times seven times
six, over three times two times one.

84
00:03:15,851 --> 00:03:18,374
Three hundred and thirty six over six.

85
00:03:19,124 --> 00:03:21,018
So there are fifty six ways.

86
00:03:21,668 --> 00:03:23,129
Now, the exam trap.

87
00:03:23,479 --> 00:03:27,567
Many students use n P r here, and
write three hundred and thirty six.

88
00:03:27,967 --> 00:03:31,993
But that counts every group
six times, once for each order.

89
00:03:31,993 --> 00:03:33,833
A group is just a group.

90
00:03:33,833 --> 00:03:37,594
Divide by three factorial,
and the answer is fifty six.

91
00:03:38,244 --> 00:03:39,439
Where is this asked?

92
00:03:39,439 --> 00:03:43,528
In JEE Main, as questions on choosing
a team or a committee with a condition.

93
00:03:44,128 --> 00:03:45,260
Exam level now.

94
00:03:45,260 --> 00:03:48,782
A team of four is chosen
from six boys and four girls.

95
00:03:48,782 --> 00:03:50,960
It must have at least two girls.

96
00:03:50,960 --> 00:03:52,093
How many teams?

97
00:03:52,093 --> 00:03:53,273
Pause and solve.

98
00:03:58,673 --> 00:04:00,014
The answer is A.

99
00:04:00,014 --> 00:04:01,771
One hundred and fifteen.

100
00:04:02,271 --> 00:04:04,165
Two girls and two boys.

101
00:04:04,165 --> 00:04:07,853
Four C two times six C
two is six times fifteen.

102
00:04:07,853 --> 00:04:08,753
Ninety.

103
00:04:09,088 --> 00:04:10,948
Three girls and one boy.

104
00:04:10,948 --> 00:04:14,412
Four C three times six
C one is four times six.

105
00:04:14,412 --> 00:04:15,443
Twenty four.

106
00:04:15,943 --> 00:04:16,843
Four girls.

107
00:04:16,791 --> 00:04:18,173
Four C four is one.

108
00:04:18,673 --> 00:04:22,556
Ninety plus twenty four plus
one is one hundred and fifteen.

109
00:04:23,156 --> 00:04:24,697
Here is your recap.

110
00:04:24,697 --> 00:04:26,279
Screenshot this card.

111
00:04:29,479 --> 00:04:31,666
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

112
00:04:32,066 --> 00:04:32,966
One.

113
00:04:32,382 --> 00:04:35,098
Why is choosing Meera and Sana the
same as choosing Sana and Meera?

114
00:04:35,998 --> 00:04:36,898
Two.

115
00:04:36,461 --> 00:04:37,587
Find six C two.

116
00:04:38,487 --> 00:04:39,387
Three.

117
00:04:39,147 --> 00:04:42,697
Why is n C r equal to
n P r over r factorial?

118
00:04:43,797 --> 00:04:45,876
Next time: arrange, or choose?

119
00:04:45,876 --> 00:04:48,057
One question decides it every time.

120
00:04:48,057 --> 00:04:49,776
We put them side by side.
